          <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-slate-500">
            Sources &amp; standards: the drawdown equation is <strong>Boyle's law</strong> applied to the
            pre-charged air volume, computed here on absolute pressures with {ATMOSPHERIC_PSI} psi of
            atmosphere added. Shell sizes are common stocked capacities and the minimum run times are
            <strong> pump-industry convention, not code</strong> — the <a
              class="underline hover:text-blue-600"
              href="https://codes.iccsafe.org/codes/i-codes"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener">IPC</a
            > governs the potable system downstream of the tank, not how the well equipment is proportioned. Real
            diaphragm tanks deliver slightly under the theoretical figure, so confirm against the manufacturer's
            published drawdown table. Constant-pressure and variable-speed systems are sized differently.
          </p>
        </div>
